WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.129
Film Courage: Well Dan, we’ve pulled a few
comments left on some of our Film Courage

00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:07.529
Youtube channel videos and we wanted to run
them by you to see what you think (because

00:00:07.529 --> 00:00:11.930
they do have valid points).

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:16.340
One comments is: “Anyone who says there
is not a formulaic process in mainstream Hollywood

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:19.890
movies (and movies in general) is kidding
themselves.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:25.640
We expect a certain story structure based
on a thousand years of Western storytelling.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.430
Deviate at your own risk!”

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:33.370
Dan Calvisi: I would agree with that except
that it’s not a formula, it’s a form.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.050
A formula would dictate what you write.

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:42.870
A form is dictating how you structure it,
at what point do you reveal things, at what

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:48.760
point should the story keep moving forward
and keep flowing rather than stop dead in

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:52.940
its tracks or just have an 8-page dialogue
scene?

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.440
So it’s not formula, it’s form.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.100
And I constantly remind my clients about that
and my students.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.800
It’s really important to understand that.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:02.940
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.500
I like that.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.510
Another comment “No wonder people are dissatisfied
with Hollywood.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:13.940
Every movie is similar and so predictable
that the industry has committed the biggest

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.010
sin.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.990
It is so predictable that it bores one to
tears.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:23.140
The story plot lines really are business plans
in disguise instead of entertainment.”

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:30.620
Dan Calvisi: That can be true and I do see
that being true in more and more Hollywood

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.330
movies (the franchise films).

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:39.460
They are very formulaic and they are employing
the same character arcs, the same themes.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:43.890
Like in animation, it’s the theme of ‘Believe
in yourself.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.200
You can do anything.’

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.979
And I think it’s more interesting when you
see an animated film that is a little more

00:01:50.979 --> 00:01:51.979
complex than that.

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:54.490
It’s a little more complicated than that.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:02:01.650
So I do like to see other themes explored
and a little bit more intelligent exploration

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.630
of character and theme.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:09.450
I do agree that a lot of the franchise films
are becoming similar because they are using

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:17.360
similar character arcs and similar themes
and similar plot devices.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.890
A lot of the action movies today I am just
so bored by because it’s just battle after

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:25.440
battle even something like the movie LOGAN.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:31.190
LOGAN was very different from a lot of other
superhero films and I think the audience responded

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:32.190
because of that.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:37.870
There was more character, it was darker, it
was more mature but once he starts slashing

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:40.950
in those action scenes, I honestly get a little
bored.

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:47.430
I’ve seen Wolverine slash with his claws
in what, 7 or 8 movies by now (maybe more)?

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:53.300
So those long extended action scenes and battle
scenes and car chases for me don’t impress

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.200
me so much because I’ve seen them in so
many movies.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:03.620
Now maybe for 12, 14, 15-year-olds, it is
more interesting and that is the core audience.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:10.090
A screenwriter shouldn’t necessarily be
focused on that 14, 15-year-old boy audience

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:15.540
because they already have a bunch of highly-paid
writers in Hollywood who will write those

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.760
particular franchise films.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:23.850
A screenwriter breaks in because they have
great characters, great dialogue and they

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:26.090
explore themes in interesting ways.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:29.810
And they come up with a new and unique take
on a commercially proven genre.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:31.770
That’s what you want to do.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.160
You want to blow away the reader.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:37.780
You want to write a great piece of material
so that you’ll get your foot in the door

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:40.810
and then they’ll hire you to do those assignments.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:47.480
But they don’t necessarily want a new writer
to write a huge franchise film for no other

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:53.060
reason because those cost so much, they’re
not going to entrust a newbie with a 100 million

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:54.709
dollar movie.

00:03:54.709 --> 00:03:58.540
And even if it’s a lower budget film, you
don’t have to write for the lowest common

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:01.540
denominator, you can write to the top of your
intelligence.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:06.770
Film Courage: Don’t you have a book that
you’ve written about creating a super hero

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:07.770
script?

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:13.420
Dan Calvisi: I have a webinar that I’ve
written called ‘Writing the Super Hero Movie.’

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.760
And yes, I’m a huge fan of super hero films.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:22.320
I like them when they really stand out with
some unique character development and interesting

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.320
themes.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:24.659
I love most of the Marvel movies.

00:04:24.659 --> 00:04:28.580
They are getting a little more formulaic in
my opinion.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:36.259
But familiar in the themes and action scenes
now.

00:04:36.259 --> 00:04:40.780
But I do love the super hero genre and I’m
always hoping that a writer will do something

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:41.860
new with it.

